the trial has begun in paris of 20  people suspected of aiding and abetting  the terror attacks that shook the city  and the country to its very core six  years ago 130 people were killed in  attacks at bars a concert hall and a  football stadium a convoy of vehicles  carrying some of the accused arrived at  the court complex where the trial is  taking place they include the man who is  believed to be the sole surviving  attacker salah abdeslam  the court case is of national  significance for the survivors of the  attacks it brings back very painful  memories dw's lisa louis filed this  report  on the 13th of november 2015 sebastian  dusey was at his job as a bartender at  la bombier  when his workplace suddenly resembled a  battlefield  at 9 30 that night i thought i was  hearing firecrackers i didn't understand  what was happening suddenly everybody  was running one person had been hit at  the leg and was trying to climb these  stairs  then i looked outside and saw the  attackers firing their kalashnikovs it  was like in the movie i threw myself on  the floor and stayed there for a while  at the time we didn't know that this was  just one out of a whole series of  attacks  that night terrorists attacked several  other bars across paris  like la belle keep two kilometers  further south east  [Music]  i used to work in this area and know  people who were celebrating their  birthday at this bar that night i have  an italian friend phil whose girlfriend  michelle was at the party yet proposed  to her just two months earlier and we  thought she survived but the next day we  found out that she was killed in the  attack  on that  she was an angel she was beautiful  intelligent kind-hearted we all miss her  for a long time i wasn't able to come  back here and read her name  even during the commemorations it's  90 of the 130 people killed that night  were at the bataclan music hall where  the attackers held hundreds of them  hostage for hours  [Music]  so you were supposed to be here that  night  yes i wanted to go to the bataclan that  night but me and my now ex-girlfriend  who was also working at a bar weren't  able to get time off that friday  the series of attacks that hit the heart  of sebastian's neighborhood still leaves  him feeling vulnerable  i sense that i should maybe keep my  distance from the court case i don't  think it would be good for me and there  are enough other people involved in the  trial  and suddenly it hits him  that it could have ended differently  you know i've just realized that it  could quite easily have been my name on  this commemorative plaque too  that hurts  sebastian says he wants to move away to  leave paris and the reminders of what  happened that night behind  all of france is transfixed transfixed  by this trial dw's of correspondent lisa  louis  joins us now from the french capital  elise i understand you were in the  courtroom today for the opening  proceedings can you share with us what  happened  well yes i was one of about two dozen  journalists allowed in during the  beginning of the trial it's very much  standard procedure what's happening here  today and tomorrow and actually this  week but this standard procedure was  somehow interrupted when the president  of the court who talked to every single  accused one by one started to talk to  salat islam the main accused who is  believed to have driven two of the  several of the uh suicide attackers to  paris that night salam when asked for  his name and for his job he said first  of all i want to state that allah is the  only god and i have left behind my jobs  to become become a warrior of islamic  state and that really showed a lot of  people here that were hoping for him to  show some remorse that that is probably  going to be very unlikely during this  trial here and this makes it of course  more significant because he has refused  to speak up to now  absolutely he's hardly been talking just  at the very beginning talking a little  bit i understand to the investigators  but since then  he has been staying silent he has been  talking to his lawyers obviously but you  know investigators are still trying to  find out why that night he did not  actually detonate his explosive belt but  he left it behind in paris and then  turned back to brussels  elisa a lot of superlatives are being  used to describe this moment what makes  this trial so historic  it's a historic tribe because it's huge  it's really a mammoth trial paris france  has built a specific  courtroom for this invested 8 million  euros  it will go on for nine months and there  are 1 800 civil plaintiffs hundreds of  lawyers  people will have weeks to talk about  what they had to go through their trauma  that night but also about the people  they lost one lady actually in advance  she said it's very important for me to  come forward and talk about my daughter  who was killed that night and showed the  attackers that my daughter was not just  part of an anonymous crowd no she was  someone i loved with a real great  character and i want to show them that  they killed people and not just  anonymous people there so this is going  to be very important for civil  plaintiffs but also for the whole of  france because it is the democratic  answer to a horrific act and france  wants to show that yes they were  attacked maybe caught unawares but they  will answer to this horrific attack in a  democratic democratic way  in conclusion lisa the trial is expected  to last around nine months that's quite  lengthy why  well because there are as i said 1 800  civil plaintiffs hundreds of them at  least 300 will talk about their version  of the event and mind you the  investigation has already quite a large  volume actually about five more than 500  dos years that investigators have  gathered over the past few years and  there might be more added to these  dossiers they will ask lots of questions  to at the accused but there will also be  lots of witnesses such as for example  former interior minister bernard kazner  and former head of state here in france  franco alond who was president at the  time of the attack so people are really  digging into this case and they want to  try they're trying to find out what  happened that day and obviously  everybody is kind of hoping that the  accused will come forward give some  explanation maybe as i said show some  remorse  lisa louis dw correspondent in paris  thank you for your reporting
 

